<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Philippines</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – present · capital Manila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Archipelagic republic independent since 1946, Asia's largest Catholic-majority country and the oldest democracy in South-East Asia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Philippines became independent from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> on 4 July 1946, ending the transition set out for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commonwealth-of-the-philippines">Commonwealth of the Philippines</a>. Its first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-roxas">Manuel Roxas</a>, took office amid wartime devastation, and the new republic signed trade and military base agreements that kept it closely tied to America.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A presidential system modelled on the American constitution faced the communist-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hukbalahap">Hukbalahap</a> rising, defeated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-magsaysay">Ramon Magsaysay</a> in the 1950s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> imposed martial law from 1972, ruling through cronies until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/people-power-revolution">People Power Revolution</a> of 1986 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corazon-aquino">Corazon Aquino</a> to power. Politics remains dominated by powerful families, while millions of Filipinos work abroad.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The 1987 constitution restored democratic institutions, and American bases closed in 1992. A long Muslim insurgency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangsamoro">Bangsamoro</a> autonomous region in 2019. The country today faces disputes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/philippines.jpg" alt="Philippines" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Philippines.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Manila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuel Roxas, Ferdinand Marcos, Corazon Aquino</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Filipino, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of the Philippines, Third Philippine Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1986&amp;focus=philippines" aria-label="Philippines on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7989 1634 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Philippines lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8309 1890L8309 1890"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8309 1890h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8309 1890h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8309 1890h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Manila. <a href="/atlas?year=1986&amp;focus=philippines">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
